WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:06.980
Let's take a look at NCBI's newly enhanced
Genome Data Viewer.

00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:12.960
You can now view AND analyze more than 500
eukaryotic Reference Sequence, or RefSeq,

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:13.970
genomes.

00:00:13.970 --> 00:00:18.640
Take the next 2 minutse to discover the GDV's
versatility.

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:24.869
On the viewer's home page you may find your
organism of interest, or use a common or scientific

00:00:24.869 --> 00:00:31.939
name to quickly search for an organism or
explore what other organisms are available.

00:00:31.939 --> 00:00:39.821
You can begin your analysis of a particular
assembly by entering a gene, a location, a

00:00:39.821 --> 00:00:45.989
phenotype, or even an NCBI accession number.

00:00:45.989 --> 00:00:51.060
Once in the viewer, you have all of the features
of the Sequence viewer, including, numerous

00:00:51.060 --> 00:01:02.210
zoom controls, and extensive Track configuration,
where you can load pre-defined sets of tracks,

00:01:02.210 --> 00:01:09.110
either NCBI Recommended Track Sets for specific
topics, or save and manage your own sets of

00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:12.260
tracks within your My NCBI account.

00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:17.590
There is also a Configuration menu for choosing
among hundreds of tracks that we provide,

00:01:17.590 --> 00:01:23.360
including expression tracks of RNA-seq data
and many new variation tracks.

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:27.111
And don't forget that you can upload your
own data either here, or

00:01:27.111 --> 00:01:31.530
use the Your data widget on the left
to add a track of aligned data from a file

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:39.740
on your local machine or at a URL.

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:43.700
Your new track appears in the Sequence Viewer,
allowing you to compare it with the wealth

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:46.590
of NCBI tracks.

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:51.520
We also recently added a BLAST function, allowing
you to see recent BLAST alignments in the

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:53.659
viewer.

00:01:53.659 --> 00:01:57.509
That was just a sampling of the Genome Data
Viewer's capabilities.

00:01:57.509 --> 00:02:11.930
Look for links to the GDV on many NCBI record
pages, including Assembly, Genome, GEO, Gene,

00:02:11.930 --> 00:02:13.270
and coming soon to BLAST.

